1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,921
Slide an empty box.

2
00:00:01,921 --> 00:00:02,821
Easy.

3
00:00:02,493 --> 00:00:03,964
Fill it with books.

4
00:00:03,964 --> 00:00:04,955
Much harder.

5
00:00:04,955 --> 00:00:06,477
On ice, it glides.

6
00:00:06,877 --> 00:00:09,040
What decides how big friction is?

7
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:13,370
Aarav drags a carton of record
books down the college corridor.

8
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:15,578
Twice the books, twice the pull.

9
00:00:15,928 --> 00:00:18,184
Then he stands it on its narrow end.

10
00:00:18,184 --> 00:00:19,645
Less floor is touching.

11
00:00:19,645 --> 00:00:21,482
Yet the pull does not change.

12
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:22,382
Why?

13
00:00:22,436 --> 00:00:24,320
One number explains it all.

14
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,194
The coefficient of friction.

15
00:00:26,794 --> 00:00:29,234
Last time we met two kinds of friction.

16
00:00:29,584 --> 00:00:32,428
The most static friction
can give, f s max.

17
00:00:32,428 --> 00:00:35,159
And kinetic friction, f k, while sliding.

18
00:00:35,559 --> 00:00:37,262
Both depend on two things.

19
00:00:37,262 --> 00:00:41,192
How hard the surfaces press
together, and what they are made of.

20
00:00:41,592 --> 00:00:44,479
How hard they press is
the normal force, N.

21
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:47,072
On a level floor, N equals the weight.

22
00:00:47,722 --> 00:00:49,210
To the motion notebook.

23
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:53,691
A twenty newton wooden block on a wooden
floor, pulled by a spring balance.

24
00:00:54,041 --> 00:00:54,960
Pull slowly.

25
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:59,055
The reading climbs, and the block
starts to slide at ten newtons.

26
00:00:59,055 --> 00:01:01,235
Sliding steadily, it reads eight.

27
00:01:01,635 --> 00:01:03,497
Add a twenty newton weight.

28
00:01:03,497 --> 00:01:04,713
N is now forty.

29
00:01:04,713 --> 00:01:08,036
The block starts at twenty,
and slides at sixteen.

30
00:01:08,436 --> 00:01:09,520
Add one more.

31
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:10,504
N is sixty.

32
00:01:10,504 --> 00:01:13,756
It starts at thirty, and
slides at twenty four.

33
00:01:14,156 --> 00:01:18,015
On the graph, the points fall on
two straight lines through zero.

34
00:01:18,015 --> 00:01:19,980
Double N, and friction doubles.

35
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:21,519
Slow-mo replay.

36
00:01:21,519 --> 00:01:25,184
Freeze, just as the heaviest
block starts to slide.

37
00:01:25,534 --> 00:01:28,560
The pull is thirty
newtons, and N is sixty.

38
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:32,145
Thirty over sixty is zero
point five, for every load.

39
00:01:32,795 --> 00:01:34,650
Now the rule, step by step.

40
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:35,922
Step one.

41
00:01:35,922 --> 00:01:38,305
f s max is proportional to N.

42
00:01:38,305 --> 00:01:40,935
So f s max equals mu s times N.

43
00:01:41,685 --> 00:01:42,585
Step two.

44
00:01:42,537 --> 00:01:45,720
In the same way, f k equals mu k times N.

45
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:47,549
Step three.

46
00:01:47,549 --> 00:01:50,002
So mu s is f s max over N.

47
00:01:50,002 --> 00:01:51,984
Here, zero point five.

48
00:01:51,984 --> 00:01:54,413
And mu k is zero point four.

49
00:01:55,163 --> 00:01:56,110
Step four.

50
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:58,113
Newtons divided by newtons.

51
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:00,976
The units cancel, so mu has no unit.

52
00:02:01,726 --> 00:02:02,626
Step five.

53
00:02:02,615 --> 00:02:06,882
Starting is harder than sliding,
so mu s is bigger than mu k.

54
00:02:07,632 --> 00:02:08,795
Remember this.

55
00:02:08,795 --> 00:02:10,858
Friction equals mu times N.

56
00:02:10,858 --> 00:02:13,487
And mu depends on the two surfaces.

57
00:02:14,187 --> 00:02:15,338
Side by side.

58
00:02:15,338 --> 00:02:17,310
The same block, N sixty.

59
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:19,953
On wood, mu k is zero point four.

60
00:02:19,953 --> 00:02:22,671
Kinetic friction, twenty four newtons.

61
00:02:23,071 --> 00:02:26,494
On ice, mu k might be
just zero point zero five.

62
00:02:26,494 --> 00:02:28,230
Friction, three newtons.

63
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:30,177
Same N, different surfaces.

64
00:02:30,777 --> 00:02:31,852
Now you try.

65
00:02:31,852 --> 00:02:34,536
Aarav doubles the books in his carton.

66
00:02:34,536 --> 00:02:36,207
What happens to mu k?

67
00:02:36,207 --> 00:02:37,543
Pause and decide.

68
00:02:40,743 --> 00:02:42,054
It stays the same!

69
00:02:42,054 --> 00:02:44,420
Friction doubles, but N doubles too.

70
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:46,739
Their ratio, mu, does not change.

71
00:02:47,389 --> 00:02:48,734
Now Aarav's puzzle.

72
00:02:48,734 --> 00:02:51,081
Why does the narrow end not help?

73
00:02:51,081 --> 00:02:52,174
Link by link.

74
00:02:52,524 --> 00:02:55,286
Surfaces really touch only at tiny bumps.

75
00:02:55,686 --> 00:02:59,324
On the narrow end, each bump is
pressed harder, and squashes more.

76
00:02:59,724 --> 00:03:03,137
On the wide face, there are
more bumps, each pressed gently.

77
00:03:03,537 --> 00:03:07,490
Either way, the true area in
contact comes out about the same.

78
00:03:07,490 --> 00:03:09,850
It is set by N, not by the face.

79
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:11,400
Your turn.

80
00:03:11,375 --> 00:03:15,818
On its wide face, the carton feels
twenty four newtons of friction.

81
00:03:15,818 --> 00:03:17,216
On its narrow end?

82
00:03:17,216 --> 00:03:18,502
Pause and decide.

83
00:03:21,702 --> 00:03:23,490
Still twenty four newtons!

84
00:03:23,490 --> 00:03:27,046
Same N, same surfaces, so
mu times N is the same.

85
00:03:27,046 --> 00:03:30,232
Friction does not depend
on the area of contact.

86
00:03:30,882 --> 00:03:32,617
Now, one worked example.

87
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:34,643
A five kilogram block.

88
00:03:34,643 --> 00:03:39,692
A spring balance reads twenty four point
five newtons as it starts to slide,

89
00:03:39,692 --> 00:03:42,284
and nineteen point six while sliding.

90
00:03:42,284 --> 00:03:43,929
Find mu s and mu k.

91
00:03:44,429 --> 00:03:45,694
N equals m g.

92
00:03:45,694 --> 00:03:49,278
Five times nine point
eight, forty nine newtons.

93
00:03:50,028 --> 00:03:53,630
mu s is twenty four point
five over forty nine.

94
00:03:53,630 --> 00:03:55,015
Zero point five.

95
00:03:55,765 --> 00:03:59,164
mu k is nineteen point
six over forty nine.

96
00:03:59,164 --> 00:04:00,586
Zero point four.

97
00:04:01,336 --> 00:04:03,957
Add five more kilograms, and N doubles.

98
00:04:03,957 --> 00:04:06,935
So the block now starts
at forty nine newtons.

99
00:04:07,585 --> 00:04:09,051
Now, the exam trap.

100
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:13,519
Many students use f equals mu
s N for every block at rest.

101
00:04:13,919 --> 00:04:16,269
But mu s N is only the maximum.

102
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:21,311
With N sixty, a gentle ten newton push
meets just ten newtons of friction.

103
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:23,321
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:27,513
In finding mu from readings or a
graph, and friction for a new load.

105
00:04:28,113 --> 00:04:29,352
Exam level now.

106
00:04:29,352 --> 00:04:34,175
A two kilogram block slides on a
level floor at six metres per second.

107
00:04:34,175 --> 00:04:37,221
mu k is zero point three, and g is ten.

108
00:04:37,221 --> 00:04:39,985
How far does it slide before it stops?

109
00:04:39,985 --> 00:04:41,277
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:46,677 --> 00:04:48,178
The answer is B.

111
00:04:48,178 --> 00:04:49,250
Six metres.

112
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:51,633
Friction is mu k m g.

113
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:55,480
Zero point three times two
times ten, six newtons.

114
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:59,674
a is six over two, three
metres per second squared.

115
00:04:59,674 --> 00:05:01,426
That is mu k times g.

116
00:05:01,426 --> 00:05:02,503
Mass cancels.

117
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:05,105
v squared equals two a s.

118
00:05:05,105 --> 00:05:07,082
Thirty six equals six s.

119
00:05:07,082 --> 00:05:08,778
So s is six metres.

120
00:05:09,378 --> 00:05:10,837
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:10,837 --> 00:05:12,336
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:15,536 --> 00:05:17,466
Can you answer these without help?

123
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:18,766
One.

124
00:05:18,361 --> 00:05:21,263
Why does twice the load
need twice the pull?

125
00:05:22,163 --> 00:05:23,063
Two.

126
00:05:22,693 --> 00:05:24,628
Why does mu have no unit?

127
00:05:25,528 --> 00:05:26,428
Three.

128
00:05:26,207 --> 00:05:29,767
Does a box slide more
easily on a smaller face?

129
00:05:29,767 --> 00:05:30,667
Why?

130
00:05:31,434 --> 00:05:33,716
Next time: tilt the floor slowly.

131
00:05:33,716 --> 00:05:36,346
At one angle, the block suddenly slips.

132
00:05:36,346 --> 00:05:37,979
What decides that angle?

133
00:05:37,979 --> 00:05:39,207
Limiting friction.
